          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Traditionally, the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            are defined – given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            – by applying the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            (
            %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}> this prop.
            ) to the monomial terms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            and deriving from that
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            for how to weigh each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"} probability amplitude
            – its
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
            – subject to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            choices.
          %p
            In contrast, in what is called the
            %em worldline formalism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            this assignment is obtained instead more conceptually as the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of a 1-dimensional QFT that lives
            %em on
            the Feynman graphs, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            theory (usually a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            into the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            that are the quanta of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in the field theory.
          %p
            One may think of this as realizing the intuition that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            correspond to worldlines of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
          %p
            Mathematically, the key step here is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mellin+transform"} Mellin transform
            – introducing a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger+parameter"}> Schwinger parameter
            ” – which realizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_F(x,y)
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            travelling from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            This worldline formalism is equivalent to the traditional formulation. It has the conceptual advantage that it expresses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            manifestly as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of its particle content given explicitly as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
            of all 1-particle processes, and the calculational advantage of automatically summing over subsets of Feynman diagrams related by exchange of external legs, thus maintaining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            and explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariance
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %p
            The worldline formulation of QFT has an evident generalization to higher dimensional
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            of “higher dimensional particles” (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"}> branes
            ”) over all possible worldvolume geometries. Indeed, for 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional"} dimensional
            branes this is precisely the way in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is defined: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            are given by the analogous “worldsheet formalism” known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            as the sum over all surfaces of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of of a 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFT"} SCFT
            of central charge 15.
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                    field theory
                  %th
                    of fundamental
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
                  %th
                    and their
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
                  %th
                    which in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
                    is given by
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
          %p
            Indeed, after decades of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            %em via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            in (
            %a{:href => "#BernKosower91"}> Bern-Kosower 91
            ), by looking at the point particle limit of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/StringFeynmanDiagrams.png", :width => "300"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "https://benjaminjurke.com/content/articles/2010/string-theory/"} Jurke 10
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/PointParticleLimitOfStringDiagram.png", :width => "300"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Schubert96"} Schubert 96
          %p
            Then (
            %a{:href => "#Strassler92"}> Strassler 92
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Strassler93"} Strassler 93
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particles"} relativistic particles
            on their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
          %p
            %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/worldlineformalismoverview.jpg"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from (
              %a{:href => "#SchmidtSchubert94"}> Schmidt-Schubert 94
              )
          %p
            The first calculation along these lines was actually done earlier in (
            %a{:href => "#MetsaevTseytlin88"}> Metsaev-Tseytlin 88
            ) where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-loop"} 1-loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            for pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            was obtained as the point-particle limit of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            in a Yang-Mills
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nepomechie83"} Nepomechie 83
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional+regularization"} dimensional regularization
            then this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            coefficient of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            vanishes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            26. This of course is the critical dimension of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particle"} virtual particle
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "string+theory+FAQ#RelationshipBetweenQuantumFieldTheoryAndStringTheory"} string theory FAQ – What is the relationship between quantum field theory and string theory?
          %h2#references References
          %p Precursor observations include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Feynman"}> Richard Feynman
                , Appendix of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical formulation of the quantum theory of electromagnetic interaction
            %li#Nepomechie83
              %p
                R.I. Nepomechie,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Remarks on quantized Yang-Mills theory in 26 dimensions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(83)90385-4"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(83)90385-4
            %li#MetsaevTseytlin88
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ruslan+Metsaev"}> Ruslan Metsaev
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arkady+Tseytlin"} Arkady Tseytlin
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On loop corrections to string theory effective actions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(88)90306-9"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(88)90306-9
          %p
            The worldline formalism as such was first derived from the point-particle limit of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            in
          %ul
            %li#BernKosower91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zvi+Bern"}> Zvi Bern
                , D. Kosower,
                %em Efficient calculation of one-loop QCD amplitudes
                Phys. Rev. D 66 (1991),(
                %a{:href => "http://prl.aps.org/abstract/PRL/v66/i13/p1669_1"}> journal
                )
            %li#BernKosower92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zvi+Bern"}> Zvi Bern
                , D. Kosower,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The computation of loop amplitudes in gauge theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://ccdb4fs.kek.jp/cgi-bin/img_index?9108076"} journal
          %p Then it was related to actual worldline quantum field theory in
          %ul
            %li#Strassler92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Strassler"}> Matthew Strassler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Field Theory Without Feynman Diagrams: One-Loop Effective Actions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9205205"} arXiv:hep-ph/9205205
            %li#Strassler93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Strassler"}> Matthew Strassler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Bern-Kosower Rules and Their Relation to Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/364079?ln=de"} spires
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#SchmidtSchubert94
              %p
                M. G. Schmidt,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Schubert"} Christian Schubert
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Worldline Path Integral Approach to Feynman Graphs
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9412358"} arXiv:hep-ph/9412358
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/381745"} inSpire:381745
            %li#Schubert96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Schubert"}> Christian Schubert
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to the Worldline Technique for Quantum Field Theory Calculations
                = succeed ")" do
